
---

# Distance Vector Routing – Part 2 and Part 3

This repository contains my implementation of Part 2 and the integration in Part 3 for the Distance Vector (DV) routing protocol used in the networking lab (Learning Flow 4a).

Part 1 (neighbour detection) was implemented earlier on a separate branch.
Part 2 and Part 3 are implemented here.

---

## Part 1 (background)

Part 1 is responsible for the HELLO protocol.
Each router sends a HELLO message every 5 seconds and removes neighbours that have not sent anything for 10 seconds.
It maintains a neighbour table but does not do any routing by itself.

Part 3 later uses this to know which neighbours exist.

---

## Part 2 – Distance Table and Distance Vector Logic

Part 2 implements the actual DV algorithm. The main components are:

### Distance Table

For each destination IP we keep a list of possible routes, each route containing:

* the neighbour used to reach the destination (“via”)
* the cost for that route (hop-count)

The structure allows storing several possible next-hops for the same destination.

### Generating a Distance Vector

The function `getDistanceVector()` builds a DV message in the required format, for example:

```
192.168.1.1:DV:(192.168.1.2,1):(192.168.1.3,2):
```

The string is dynamically allocated and can be sent directly by Part 3.

### Processing a Received Distance Vector

The function `processDistanceVector()` parses an incoming DV message, updates the table, and checks whether the best route to any destination has changed.
If it has changed, the function `dvUpdate()` is called to notify Part 3 that a new DV needs to be sent.

### Helper Functions

Part 2 also provides:

* `dvSent()` – called after a DV is actually transmitted
* internal route update functions
* mutex protection for the table

---

## Compiling Part 2

To build the normal Part 2 module:

```
make
```

To build a version with the internal test main enabled:

```
make test
```

This produces `part2_test`.

---

## Part 3 – Full Integration

Part 3 combines the neighbour detection (HELLO protocol) with the distance vector logic from Part 2.
It contains:

* the sender thread (periodically sends HELLO messages)
* the receiver thread (receives HELLO and DV messages)
* the timeout thread (detects dead neighbours)
* integration code that updates the distance table whenever links appear or disappear
* code that sends a DV whenever Part 2 reports that something changed

The main file for Part 3 is `part3.c`.

### Compiling Part 3

You can compile the integrated router using:

```
make core
```

or:

```
make router_core
```

This produces the executable:

```
router_core
```

---

## Running Everything (using the provided script)

The file `run_core.sh` sets up several Linux network namespaces, connects them with virtual interfaces, and starts multiple instances of the router so they behave like separate machines.

To run the whole system:

```
sudo ./run_core.sh
```

This will automatically create three router namespaces and start the program in each one.
The routers will start exchanging HELLO messages and distance vectors on their own.

To inspect a router’s routing table inside a namespace:

```
sudo ip netns exec r1 ip route
```

(Replace r1 with r2 or r3.)

---

## Note About Limitations

This project does not implement loop-prevention techniques such as split horizon or poison reverse.
If a router is simply killed or closed, the remaining routers may temporarily advertise outdated routes, which can cause the classic “count-to-infinity” behaviour.
This is normal and acceptable for this assignment.

